Allocators: where every allocating type of ca gets its memory. Each container, string and
owner is templated on its allocator (`A = ca::heap`), so the same code runs on the general heap, in
an arena, or on fresh pages.

| Allocator | Kind | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `ca::heap` | stateless | the default: per-thread size classes, no lock on a thread's own blocks |
| `ca::page_allocator` | stateless | whole pages per block, zeroed, grown by remapping without copying |
| `ca::libc_allocator` | stateless | malloc, realloc and free: the baseline benchmarks compare with |
| `ca::arena_allocator` | stateful | blocks of a `ca::arena`, released all at once |
| `ca::arena_scope` | stateful | temporaries of a `ca::arena`, released by `pop()`; results kept through `result()` |

### The allocator contract

An allocator (`ca::is_allocator`) is a cheap value, copied without failing, with two members:

- `allocate(size, alignment) const -> result<allocation>`: a block of at least size bytes; the
  `allocation` returned carries the address and the usable size, which may be larger.
- `deallocate(block, alignment) const -> void`: hands a block back; never fails.

Optionally `expand` (resize in place), `reallocate` (resize, moving if needed) and
`allocate_zeroed`. The free functions `ca::expand`, `ca::reallocate` and `ca::allocate_zeroed`
use them when present and fall back to allocate, copy and deallocate otherwise, so a minimal
allocator works everywhere. The `_n` forms (`allocate_n<T>`, `reallocate_n<T>`, `expand_n<T>`,
`deallocate_n<T>`) count in objects and check the multiplication for overflow.

An allocator that declares `static constexpr bool STATELESS = true` is stateless: every instance
is interchangeable, and allocator-aware types store it in no space. Others are stateful: memory
goes back through the allocator it came from, and the types holding one have no default
constructor.

```c++
#include "ca/integers.hpp"
#include "ca/memory.hpp"
#include "ca/optional.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	const ca::heap heap{};
	auto block = ca::allocate_n<int>(heap, 10U);
	if (!block || block->size() < 10U * sizeof(int))
		return 1;
	int* const values = static_cast<int*>(block->data());
	for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i)
		values[i] = i;

	auto grown = ca::reallocate_n<int>(heap, *block, 1000U);
	if (!grown || static_cast<int*>(grown->data())[9] != 9)
		return 2;
	ca::deallocate_n<int>(heap, *grown);

	auto zeroed = ca::allocate_zeroed(ca::page_allocator{}, 10000U, 64U);
	if (!zeroed || zeroed->size() < 10000U || static_cast<ca::byte*>(zeroed->data())[9999] != 0U)
		return 3;
	ca::page_allocator{}.deallocate(*zeroed, 64U);
	return 0;
}
```

### Arenas

`ca::arena` owns chunks of pages and hands out blocks by bumping a pointer: allocation is a few
instructions, and `cleanup()` releases everything at once, or `reset()` empties it for reuse. It is
an allocator resource (`ca::is_allocator_resource`): not an allocator itself, it gives out
`arena_allocator`s, one pointer wide, which must not outlive it. Deallocating only gives memory
back when the block is the newest one. Arenas are not thread-safe.

```c++
#include "ca/dynamic_array.hpp"
#include "ca/make_in.hpp"
#include "ca/memory.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto made = ca::arena::make();
	if (!made)
		return 1;
	ca::arena& arena = *made;

	ca::dynamic_array<int, ca::arena_allocator> squares{arena.allocator()};
	for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i)
		if (!squares.append(i * i))
			return 2;

	auto other = ca::make_in<ca::dynamic_array<int>>(arena);
	if (!other || !other->append(42))
		return 3;

	const bool ok = squares[999] == 999 * 999 && (*other)[0] == 42;
	if (!squares.cleanup() || !other->cleanup())
		return 4;
	arena.reset();
	return ok && arena.cleanup() ? 0 : 5;
}
```

### Arena scopes

`arena.push()` opens a scope and returns it as an allocator, `ca::arena_scope` (two pointers):
what it allocates are temporaries, all released by its `pop()`, chunks included but one kept for
the next growth. What must outlive the pop is allocated, explicitly, through `scope.result()`: an
`arena_allocator` over the enclosing scope (or the arena itself), which the pop does not touch.
Nothing is copied or moved, so results may be whole structures: containers of containers, nodes
pointing at each other. `scope.push()` opens a nested scope, whose results are temporaries of
`scope`, so each level hands its results up exactly one level.

Scopes alternate between the two lanes of the arena: a scope's temporaries share a lane with its
parent's results, and its results with its parent's temporaries. The rule follows: allocate only
through the innermost scope, or through its `result()`. Allocating through an enclosing scope (or
`arena.allocator()`) while a nested scope is open puts the block where the nested pop releases it.
After `pop()`, the scope, its copies and its blocks are not used again; popping a scope pops the
scopes nested in it, and a pop whose mark is already gone traps.

```c++
#include "ca/dynamic_array.hpp"
#include "ca/make_in.hpp"
#include "ca/memory.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	auto made = ca::arena::make();
	if (!made)
		return 1;
	ca::arena& arena = *made;

	const ca::arena_scope scope = arena.push();
	auto squares = ca::make_in<ca::dynamic_array<int>>(scope);
	auto evens = ca::make_in<ca::dynamic_array<int>>(scope.result());
	if (!squares || !evens)
		return 2;
	for (int i = 0; i < 1000; ++i)
		if (!squares->append(i * i))
			return 3;
	for (const int square : *squares)
		if (square % 2 == 0 && !evens->append(square))
			return 4;
	scope.pop();

	const bool ok = evens->size() == 500U && (*evens)[499] == 998 * 998;
	return ok && arena.cleanup() ? 0 : 5;
}
```

`ca::allocator_of<R>`, `ca::as_allocator(source)` and `ca::is_allocator_source` unify the two
kinds of source, so `make_in` takes either an allocator or a resource.

### The heap

`ca::heap` serves sizes up to 128 KiB from 48 size classes (16 to 128 bytes by 16, then four per
doubling, so at most 25% above the size asked for), each thread from its own 1 MiB spans with no
lock and no atomic. A block finds its span by masking its address, so there is no per-block
header. Any thread may free any block; one freed away from its owner goes back with a single
atomic operation. Larger sizes are mapped pages, and growing them remaps without copying. The
performance notes of `heap.hpp` give the costs per operation.

`ca::heap` is one instantiation of `ca::basic_heap<Pages, G, Binding>`, the same engine with
its three dependencies as parameters, for code where mmap and threads are not the right ones
(a kernel: frames for pages, one heap per CPU, interrupts off while a heap is touched):

| Parameter | ca::heap | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `Pages` | `ca::page_allocator` | stateless allocator of spans (aligned to their size), large blocks, heap states |
| `G` | `ca::heap_geometry` | `ca::is_heap_geometry`: span size, largest class, spare spans, state chunk, `STATISTICS`, `CHECK_DOUBLE_FREE` |
| `Binding` | `ca::heap_thread_binding` | where the current heap is (`current`), what keeps others off it (`pin`, `unpin`), how a new one is installed (`adopt`) |

The fast paths are in `basic_heap.hpp`; the rest is in `impl/heap_engine.hpp`, which only the
translation unit instantiating the heap includes. With `STATISTICS`, `statistics()` sums every
heap's counters: blocks in use and spans per class, spare spans, large blocks and their bytes.
With `CHECK_DOUBLE_FREE`, a bit per 16 bytes of each span marks the blocks in use, and a block
freed twice, or an address off a 16-byte boundary, traps, in every build.

```c++
#include "ca/memory.hpp"
#include "4-lifecycle/memory/impl/heap_engine.hpp"

struct small_spans final {
	static constexpr ca::usz SPAN_SIZE = 65536U;
	static constexpr ca::usz LARGEST_CLASS = 2048U;
	static constexpr ca::u32 SPARE_SPANS = 0U;
	static constexpr ca::usz STATE_CHUNK = 4096U;
	static constexpr bool STATISTICS = true;
	static constexpr bool CHECK_DOUBLE_FREE = true;
};

template <typename Heap>
struct one_heap final {
	using state = typename Heap::state_type;
	static constinit inline state* _current = Heap::empty_state();
	struct token final {
	};
	static auto current(void) noexcept -> state* { return _current; }
	static auto pin(void) noexcept -> token { return token{}; }
	static auto unpin(token) noexcept -> void {}
	static auto adopt(state* const heap) noexcept -> void { _current = heap; }
};

using my_heap = ca::basic_heap<ca::page_allocator, small_spans, one_heap>;
template class ca::basic_heap<ca::page_allocator, small_spans, one_heap>;

auto main(void) -> int {
	const my_heap heap{};
	auto block = heap.allocate(100U, 8U);
	if (!block || block->size() != 112U)
		return 1;
	const auto held = my_heap::statistics();
	if (held.small_blocks() != 1U || held.small_bytes() != 112U)
		return 2;
	heap.deallocate(*block, 8U);
	return my_heap::statistics().small_blocks() == 0U ? 0 : 3;
}
```

### Under ASan

ASan sees the pages `ca::heap` and `ca::arena` map, not the blocks they carve from them. With
`ca_memory_poisoning` (on under `-fsanitize=address`, `make SAN=address`) they mark for it what
they do not hand out: a free block of the heap, link word included, the uncarved room of a span,
a spare span; an arena chunk's room past its newest block, the padding between blocks, what a
`deallocate` or a scope's `pop()` gave back, a spare chunk. Reading or writing there is reported
as a use after poison, with the stack of the access; a pointer kept past `pop()`, or a block
allocated through an enclosing scope while a nested one is open (the arena scope rule), shows at
its next use. A heap block freed twice traps, whatever the geometry. Memory going back to the
kernel is unpoisoned first, for whoever maps the range next. Every other build compiles the
hooks to nothing.
